    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>An Ionian island state off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mycale">Mount Mycale</a>, home of the great sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> and an early Greek naval power.</li><li>Under the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a> around 530 BCE it dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and built the harbour mole, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos">Heraion</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunnel-of-eupalinos">Tunnel of Eupalinos</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> was the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epicurus">Epicurus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristarchus-of-samos">Aristarchus</a>, who argued that the earth moves around the sun.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunnel-of-eupalinos">Tunnel of Eupalinos</a>, 1,036 metres long, was dug from both ends of a mountain at once in the sixth century BCE, and the two teams met almost exactly.</p></aside>
    </section>

