    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/samo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samo.jpg" alt="Samo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N%C3%A1klo_(vrch),_S%C3%A1mo_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Palickap, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> is a fertile, mountainous island in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mycale">Mount Mycale</a> on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> by a strait little more than a kilometre and a half wide. It was inhabited in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, and a cult of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> at a marshy site near the mouth of the Imbrasos river, later the great sanctuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos">Heraion</a>, reaches back at least to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> Age. Greek tradition held that the island had earlier been settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carians">Carians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leleges">Leleges</a> and that Ionian colonists arrived under a leader named Procles from Epidaurus; whatever the details, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionian Greeks</a> were established on Samos by about 1000 BCE. Their chief town grew up on the south-east coast, on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoreio">Pythagoreio</a>, with a natural harbour facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-roman-province">Asia</a>, and Samos became one of the twelve members of the Ionian League, which met at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panionium">Panionion</a> on Mount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mycale">Mycale</a> opposite. It also held territory on the mainland, which brought it into repeated conflict with its neighbours Priene and Miletus. In the eighth century BCE the Samians built one of the earliest monumental temples in the Greek world at the Heraion, which became a rich sanctuary receiving dedications from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and a symbol of the island's identity for the rest of its history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos.jpg" alt="Heraion of Samos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos">Heraion of Samos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heraion_of_Samos_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tomisti, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> combined good farmland with an outstanding position on the sea routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Its people grew olives and vines, and Samian oil and wine were exported in their own types of amphora. The island was also a major centre of craft: Samian workshops were famous for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-working">bronze-working</a>, and tradition credited the architects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhoecus">Rhoecus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodorus-of-samos">Theodorus</a> with pioneering the casting of bronze statues. Samians traded and settled widely. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells how a merchant named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colaeus">Colaeus</a>, driven off course on the way to Egypt, reached Tartessos in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> around 638 BCE and returned with a fortune in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, part of which he dedicated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos">Heraion</a> as a great bronze bowl. Samos had its own sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naucratis</a> in Egypt, and it founded colonies such as Perinthus on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Propontis</a>, Nagidus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celenderis">Celenderis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and Minoa on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorgos">Amorgos</a>, while exiles from the island settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pozzuoli">Dicaearchia</a>, the later Puteoli, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Land on the island was long held by an aristocracy of landowners, the <em>geomoroi</em>. The Heraion stood at the centre of religious life, and Samian poets, craftsmen and thinkers made the island one of the cultural centres of archaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Herodotus also records that the fable-teller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aesop">Aesop</a> was the slave of a Samian named Iadmon.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade.jpg" alt="Battle of Lade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ionian_revolt_Battle_of_Lade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ionian_Revolt_Campaign_Map-fr.svg: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting) derivative work: MinisterForBadTimes (talk), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> was one of the earliest Greek naval powers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> records that the Corinthian shipwright <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ameinocles">Ameinocles</a> built four ships for the Samians around 700 BCE, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelantine-war">Lelantine War</a> the island is said to have sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> while its rival Miletus supported Eretria. Its naval power peaked under the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a>, who seized power around 538 BCE and built a fleet of a hundred penteconters, backed by a thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> calls him the first Greek after the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">Minos</a> to aim at mastery of the sea; he raided islands and mainland towns, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenia">Rhenaea</a> and dedicated it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a>, and defeated a Lesbian fleet sent to help Miletus. Around 525 BCE Samian exiles brought a Spartan and Corinthian army to the island, but it failed to take the city after a siege of forty days. Polycrates built a harbour mole and strong walls to protect his base. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> Samos sent sixty ships to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a> in 494 BCE, but forty-nine of them deserted before the fighting, contributing to the Ionian defeat. Later, as a ship-providing member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a>, Samos fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> itself in 440–439 BCE, when its fleet under the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melissus-of-samos">Melissus</a> won a victory before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> forced the city to surrender after a siege of nine months.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> was ruled by kings, one of whom, Amphicrates, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> mentions in connection with a war against Aegina. Power later rested with the <em>geomoroi</em>, a landowning aristocracy that was probably overthrown in the early sixth century BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a>, son of Aeaces, seized power around 538 BCE together with his brothers Pantagnotus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syloson">Syloson</a>, then killed the first and banished the second to rule alone. His court attracted the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anacreon">Anacreon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibycus">Ibycus</a> and the physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democedes">Democedes of Croton</a>, and he made alliances first with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasis-ii">Amasis of Egypt</a> and later with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II of Persia</a>. Around 522 BCE the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oroetes">Oroetes</a> lured him to the mainland and had him killed. His secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeandrius">Maeandrius</a> briefly took over until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> installed Syloson as a client tyrant, and Persian-backed tyrants, including Syloson's son Aeaces, ruled Samos until 479 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> the island was governed by an oligarchy until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> imposed a democracy in 440 BCE. A democratic revolution in 412 BCE made Samos Athens' most loyal ally, and in 405 BCE Athens granted the Samians citizenship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> restored the oligarchs in 404 BCE, and from 365 to 322 BCE the island was held by Athenian settlers, cleruchs, while the Samians themselves lived in exile.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hera"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hera.jpg" alt="Hera" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hera_Campana_Louvre_Ma2283.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marie-Lan Nguyen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> left a lasting mark through its engineering, its sanctuary and the people it produced. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> singled out three Samian works as the greatest in the Greek world: the harbour mole, the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>, which he considered the largest temple he knew, and the tunnel built by the engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eupalinos">Eupalinos of Megara</a> to bring water to the city. The tunnel, 1,036 metres long, was dug through a mountain from both ends at once, and the two teams met with only a small error, a remarkable feat of surveying that can still be walked through today. The town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoreio">Pythagoreio</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos">Heraion</a> are now together a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Samos was the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a>, who left for Croton in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, by tradition to escape the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a>, and whose followers shaped Greek mathematics and philosophy. The Eleatic philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melissus-of-samos">Melissus</a> was a Samian statesman, the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duris-of-samos">Duris</a> came from the island, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epicurus">Epicurus</a> was born there to an Athenian settler family in 341 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristarchus-of-samos">Aristarchus of Samos</a>, working in the third century BCE, proposed that the earth moves around the sun, an idea that anticipated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Copernicus</a> by some eighteen centuries. The Samian expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colaeus">Colaeus</a> to Tartessos also became one of the best-known stories of early Greek long-distance trade.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mycale"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mycale.jpg" alt="Battle of Mycale" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mycale">Battle of Mycale</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Priene_colline_colonne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">No machine-readable author provided. Pedro Lassouras assumed (based on copyright claims)., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> was worn away by more powerful neighbours. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a> the Persian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otanes">Otanes</a> conquered the island for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syloson">Syloson</a> around 517 BCE and, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, killed so many inhabitants that it had to be repopulated. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mycale">Battle of Mycale</a> in 479 BCE Samos regained its freedom, but the crushing defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 439 BCE cost it its walls, its fleet and a heavy indemnity. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> took the island in 404 BCE and restored the oligarchs, the Samians honoured him as a god, the first Greek so treated in his lifetime. In 365 BCE the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timotheus-general">Timotheus</a> expelled a Persian garrison after a siege of ten months, and Athens settled the island with its own citizens. The Samians recovered their home only in 322 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a> returned them in line with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s decree on exiles. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> Samos had its own institutions but was usually subordinate to a larger power: it served as a major Ptolemaic naval base in the third century BCE and later came within the orbit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-iii">Attalus III</a> bequeathed his kingdom to Rome, Samos became part of the province of Asia, organised in 129 BCE. It remained a prosperous island under Roman rule, and its sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> continued to attract visitors and dedications.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> was one of the great island states of archaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>: under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a> the strongest naval power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, it produced landmark engineering such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunnel-of-eupalinos">Tunnel of Eupalinos</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos">Heraion</a>, and was home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epicurus">Epicurus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristarchus-of-samos">Aristarchus</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and the Hellenistic kings absorbed it.</em></p>

